<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Christoph, Duke of Württemberg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1515">1515</a>–<a href="/years/1568">1568</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Christopher, Duke of Württemberg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Christoph was Duke of Württemberg from 1550 to 1568, succeeding his father Ulrich. With the reformer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-brenz">Johannes Brenz</a> he organised a Lutheran territorial church, set out in the Great Church Order of 1559, and created a system of schools and monastic seminaries to train clergy. He worked with the estates and was one of the most respected Protestant princes of his time.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/christoph-duke-of-wurttemberg.jpg" alt="Christoph, Duke of Württemberg" width="320" height="624" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Abraham del Hele · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abraham_Hel_001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Christoph was born on 12 May 1515, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulrich-duke-of-wurttemberg">Ulrich, Duke of Württemberg</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabina">Sabina</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. In November of that year his mother fled to her parents' court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, and he stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a> with his father and his elder sister Anna. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabian-league">Swabian League</a> raised troops against Ulrich, the children were taken to Castle Hohentübingen, and after the castle surrendered in 1519 Ulrich was banished and Württemberg came under Austrian rule.</p><p>Christoph was sent to the court of Emperor Maximilian I at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/innsbruck">Innsbruck</a>, where he grew up under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> tutelage and gained political experience, and Maximilian's successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> took him on his travels through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After Ulrich won back Württemberg from the Austrians in 1534, Christoph went to the French court and was drawn into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s wars against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>. At the end of the 1530s he became a Protestant. The Treaty of Reichenweier of 1542 made him governor of Montbéliard, a Württemberg possession, and in 1544 he married Anna Maria, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-the-pious">George, Margrave of Brandenburg-Ansbach</a>, with whom he had twelve children.</p><p>When he succeeded his father in 1550 he had to make heavy payments to Ferdinand I to avoid charges of treason. In the following years he reorganised the whole administration of church and state and reformed and supported the schools. He gave Amandenhof castle near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bad-urach">Urach</a> to Hans von Ungnad, who made it the seat of the South Slavic Bible Institute.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Christoph died on 28 December 1568. His eldest son Eberhard had died in May of that year, and he was succeeded by his son Ludwig I. His daughters married into many Protestant princely houses: Hedwig and Sabine to the landgraves of Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marburg">Marburg</a> and Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a>, Dorothea Maria to Otto Heinrich, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/count-palatine">Count Palatine</a> of Sulzbach, and Sophie to Friedrich Wilhelm I, Duke of Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Christoph, Duke of Württemberg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When Charles I died in 1576, his lands were partitioned among his sons: Eitel Friedrich IV received Hechingen and the ancestral county, Charles II received Sigmaringen and Veringen, and <b>Christoph</b> received Haigerloch.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hohenzollern">County of Hohenzollern · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Duke <b>Christoph</b> built a model Lutheran church order and school system.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-wurttemberg">Duchy of Württemberg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bad-urach">Bad Urach</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.49,9.4&amp;year=1549" aria-label="Bad Urach on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4768 469 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Christoph, Duke of Württemberg"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5218 739h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5218 739h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.49,9.4&amp;year=1549">What was here in 1549? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
